// permutation, the sponge construction with pad10*1, and the domain suffixes 01 (SHA-3) and 1111 (SHAKE). Messages are bit
// strings (least significant bit of each byte first, as in Keccak), so non-byte-aligned lengths are exercised.
// The round constants and rho offsets are the published tables of the Keccak team (data). Checks 291 published known-answer outputs, a subset of the 6 x 2048 vectors (bit lengths 0..2047) of the Keccak team's
// ShortMsgKAT files for FIPS 202: for each function the bit lengths 0..32, the lengths k*rate + {-8,-1,0,1,8} (around each
// block boundary) and 2040..2047 (selection rule fixed before running). SHA-3 digests are compared in full; SHAKE outputs
// are compared over all 512 published bytes (several rate blocks). Exit 0 only if all match. Vectors are data copied as numbers.
// Negative control: -DSHA3_SUFFIX_BAD=1 turns the SHA-3 domain suffix 01 into 11 (as if the Keccak-submission padding bits were wrong).
